=====================================
FOLDER: speciation
SYSTEM: MareNostrum 5 — glogin2
CREATED: 06/08/2026
UPDATED: 06/08/2026
PURPOSE: Thermodynamic speciation of the Mg(II) / pyrophosphate system as a
         function of pH, scanned over a 20x20 grid of total Mg and total PP
         concentrations. Supports the choice of composition for the DeePMD
         simulation boxes.
=====================================

06/08/2026

o The folder holds a 20x20 grid of independent speciation calculations, 400 in
  total. Each subfolder is named 'MMMM_PPPP', where the two four-digit blocks are
  the total Mg and the total pyrophosphate concentration in millimolar, so
  '0035_0080' means total Mg = 0.035 M and total PP = 0.080 M. Both axes run from
  0.005 M to 0.100 M in steps of 0.005 M. I verified that the folder name and the
  concentrations written inside the corresponding files agree for all 400 cases.

o The model itself is in 'speciation.py' at the top level. This top copy is a
  template: the two lines that set the totals read 'c1 = x' and 'c2 = y', and the
  generating script substitutes the numbers before dropping a frozen copy of the
  script into each subfolder. The file 'temp' sitting next to it is a leftover of
  that substitution, with 'c1' already replaced and 'c2' still symbolic. Neither
  file is a result, and 'temp' can be deleted without losing anything.

o Eleven species are considered. On the pyrophosphate side the full protonation
  ladder P2O7(4-), HP2O7(3-), H2P2O7(2-), H3P2O7(-) and H4P2O7, with
  pKa = 0.85, 1.96, 6.60 and 9.41. On the magnesium side the free ion Mg(2+), the
  solid Mg(OH)2, and the hydroxo complex MgOH(+). Three mixed complexes bridge the
  two sides: MgP2O7(2-), MgHP2O7(-) and the binuclear Mg2P2O7.

o The constants are kd_ppmg = 3.9e-6, kd_pphmg = 8.7e-4 and kd_ppmg2 = 1.78e-8
  (this last one cumulative, so Mg2P2O7 is second order in free Mg), taken from
  10.1021/ja01578a006. The solubility product of brucite is
  Ksp = 5.61e-12 from the CRC Handbook, 76th ed. The reference composition that
  inspired the grid comes from 10.1074/jbc.M110.214510. Water autoprotolysis is
  set at pKw = 14.0, i.e. 25 C throughout, and no ionic strength correction is
  applied anywhere, so every constant is a concentration constant at infinite
  dilution. At the upper end of the grid the ionic strength is a few tenths molar,
  which is where this assumption is weakest.

o Worth flagging: 'beta_mgoh' is set to 0.0, so the MgOH(+) complex is declared but
  switched off. The 'mgoh' column is identically zero in all 400 runs and the only
  sink for magnesium at high pH is the solid. If MgOH(+) is ever enabled the
  alkaline half of every curve will shift.

o Each subfolder contains the same five outputs, all tagged with its own pair of
  concentrations: 'concentrations_vs_pH_<Mg>_<PP>.dat' with absolute molarities,
  'molecular_ratios_vs_pH_<Mg>_<PP>.dat' with the same data as fractions,
  'pp_molecular_ratio_<Mg>_<PP>.png' and 'mg_molecular_ratio_<Mg>_<PP>.png' with
  the two speciation diagrams, and 'speciation_resume_<Mg>_<PP>.txt' with the
  constants, the precipitation onset and the dominant species every half pH unit.
  The pH scan is 0.00 to 13.95 in steps of 0.05, so 280 points per run.

o I re-derived the two mass balances directly from the stored '.dat' files for all
  400 runs. The largest relative closure error is 3.7e-7 on Mg and 3.8e-7 on PP,
  which is exactly the rounding of the '%.6e' output format and not a defect of the
  solver. No negative concentrations appear anywhere, and the saturation condition
  is respected. The 400 concentration files are pairwise distinct, so no grid point
  has been silently duplicated or overwritten.

o A grid-wide analysis is written in 'speciation_grid_analysis.txt', which holds the
  full 20x20 maps of precipitation onset, of the fraction of pyrophosphate carrying
  magnesium at pH 7, of the maximum fraction of Mg2P2O7, of free Mg at pH 7 and of
  the maximum fraction of MgP2O7, plus a regime map.

o On whether these are really 400 different systems: they are, but not uniformly
  across pH. Below pH 2 all 400 curves collapse onto each other, the largest spread
  in any pyrophosphate fraction being 0.0014. Magnesium binding is negligible there
  and the speciation is the bare pKa ladder. Above pH 12 they collapse again, to
  within 5e-7, because every system ends with all the magnesium in the solid and
  all the pyrophosphate as free P2O7(4-). The whole difference between the 400
  systems lives in the window between roughly pH 4 and pH 9, where the spread in a
  single fraction reaches 0.95, i.e. the full range.

o The more useful negative result is that the Mg:PP ratio alone does not fix the
  speciation. Grouping the grid into the 63 families that share a ratio, the
  fractions inside a family still differ by up to 0.45. The 1:1 diagonal is the
  clearest case: from 0.005/0.005 M to 0.100/0.100 M the peak MgP2O7 fraction
  barely moves, 0.938 to 0.941, but the peak MgHP2O7 fraction climbs from 0.415 to
  0.603, the peak Mg2P2O7 fraction from 0.084 to 0.159, and the free Mg fraction at
  pH 7 falls tenfold, from 0.246 to 0.024. Two boxes prepared at the same ratio but
  at different dilution are therefore not the same chemical system, and the
  binuclear complex is what breaks the scaling, being second order in free Mg.

o Some regularities do hold. The maximum MgP2O7 fraction is stoichiometrically
  capped: it equals the Mg:PP ratio whenever magnesium is the limiting reagent and
  saturates at 0.941 as soon as Mg >= PP, which is the clean triangular structure
  visible in that map. Precipitation onset is monotone in both totals, earliest at
  pH 8.90 for 0.100/0.005 M and latest at pH 11.75 for 0.005/0.095 M, so a 2.85 pH
  unit span across the grid; along the 1:1 diagonal it is pinned at 10.35 for every
  concentration except the most dilute point, where it is 10.40. In every one of
  the 400 systems all the magnesium is eventually in the solid by the end of the
  scan.

o Classifying the grid by how much pyrophosphate carries magnesium at pH 7 gives
  three regimes and they are well populated: 45 points below 0.25, where
  pyrophosphate stays free and protonated, 132 points between 0.25 and 0.75, and
  223 points above 0.75, where pyrophosphate is essentially magnesium saturated.
  The boundary runs diagonally, and the transition is broad enough that the grid
  spacing of 0.005 M resolves it comfortably. If a single representative
  composition is needed for the simulation boxes, the mid regime near
  0.050/0.050 M is the one where the speciation is most sensitive to the totals and
  therefore the most demanding test.
